WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.880
The sound you just heard was the entire Disney+
launch team smacking themselves in the heads

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.620
while projecting a collective "Doh!" into
the stratosphere.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:14.060
"Doh!"

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.760
You've probably already heard that the hotly
anticipated streaming service made its debut,

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.320
bringing with it all manner of titles from
the Disney vault, not to mention a slew of

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.920
properties of the Star Wars, Marvel, and Pixar
persuasion.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.610
We're pretty sure you've also heard that launch
day for Disney+ was far from perfect, with

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:33.360
many early subscribers unable to even log
on to the service.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.940
Those that were finally able to log on, and
had a hankering for some early episodes of

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.790
The Simpsons, were met with their own set
of issues when they found that those episodes

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:46.219
of the beloved animated series appeared to
be missing something: significant portions

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:46.760
of the picture.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:52.060
The problem, it seems, stemmed from Disney's
decision to screen all content on Disney+

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.600
in the widescreen aspect ratio of 16:9.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.310
On the surface, that seems like a sound choice
by the House of Mouse, as the widescreen ratio

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:03.850
is by far the most popular for home viewing;
computer monitors and flatscreen televisions

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.000
display images rendered in this aspect ratio
natively, and it's standard for HDTV broadcasts

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:09.800
and digital media.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.320
The problem lies in the fact that The Simpsons
has been on the air since before the format

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.340
was in wide use; before, in fact, consumer
flatscreen televisions even existed, and that

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.780
earlier episodes were aired in the then-standard
4:3 aspect ratio.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.640
Episodes of the series weren't produced in
the 16:9 format until its twentieth season,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.600
and when the 4:3 aspect ratio is cropped for
the newer format, a large portion of the picture

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.300
is lost.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.940
So, much to the dismay of the show's fiercely
loyal and often fanatically astute fan base,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.660
the first 19 seasons of The Simpsons are indeed
only partially streaming on Disney+.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.860
"Okay, don't panic.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.360
Marge, stop panicking!"

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.920
Now, for some shows that would not be a significant
problem.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.140
But as any Simpsons fan would be quick to
tell you, part of what's made the show so

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.020
special is that some of its best jokes are
not front and center.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.680
Rather, the creative teams behind The Simpsons
have long reveled in the chance to hide sly

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.340
in-jokes and savage social jabs in virtually
every corner of the frame.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:10.000
That's obviously a big part of what has made
The Simpsons one of the more endlessly re-watchable

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.300
shows in the history of television.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:16.220
It's also exactly why fans were so excited
at the chance to revisit some of their favorite

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.550
early moments of the iconic series on launch
day, but for now, at least, it seems that

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:25.220
those brilliantly winking comedic morsels
have been unceremoniously nixed, as Disney

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.330
made the questionable decision not to adjust
the aspect ratio for Homer and the rest of

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.540
the Simpson family's game-changing early episodes.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:36.020
"Rest assured that I was on the internet within
minutes, registering my disgust throughout

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:36.540
the world."

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.040
The decision to go ahead and screen the cropped
versions of those early episodes is even more

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.769
surprising because it seems like there's no
way that the Mouse House wouldn't have known

00:02:44.769 --> 00:02:45.500
this would be an issue.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.700
After all, extended cable outlet FXX, now
also owned by The Walt Disney Company, did

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.250
the exact same thing when it began airing
episodes of The Simpsons back in 2014, and

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.960
it also experienced the same sort of backlash
from the series' eagle-eyed fandom.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.760
It seems like it'd be pretty simple for Disney+
to remedy the issue by offering earlier seasons

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.310
in 4:3, or perhaps even giving viewers a choice
between the two aspect ratios, but so far,

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.360
Disney hasn't announced any plans to do anything
of the sort, and in fact, the entertainment

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.320
megalith has been mum on the whole uproar.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.100
While we wait to see how Disney+ addresses
its launch day Simpsons crisis, it seems that

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.760
most of the show's fans are at least trying
to take the issue in stride, if only because

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.120
they're beyond excited to stream all 30 seasons
of the series.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:32.020
Still, there is a faction of fans already
threatening to cancel their Disney+ subscriptions

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.500
if the problem is not addressed.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.370
For the time being, it seems they'll just
have to make do with watching some of the

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.000
series' best episodes without getting all
of the in-joking goodness they're used to.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.020
Maybe in the future we'll all forget this
ever happened.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.060
"Smithers, use the amnesia ray.”

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.320
“You mean the revolver, sir?”

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:47.880
“Precisely.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.460
Be sure to wipe your own memory clear when
you're finished."

